# External post drafts — kept here untracked; published on their own platforms
/articles/

# Maintainer-only tooling. What lands in the public repository is the product and
# what a user of it needs; the scripts that run the release are neither, and
# publishing them puts internal process into the distribution. Kept here so the
# maintainer still has them, untracked so nobody else receives them.
/scripts/

# Build output
/opossum
# `go build ./cmd/x` without -o writes the binary here under its package name.
# The tracked-file check catches it, but only once it is already committed — so
# every command in cmd/ is listed, not only the ones that have bitten somebody.
/busy
/changelog
/mutate
/noleftovers
/dist/

# Go
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
*.test
*.out
go.work
go.work.sum
vendor/

# Secrets — never commit a real .env (tokens, keys). examples/.env is an
# intentional, non-secret demo (interpolation values), so it stays tracked.
.env
!examples/.env

# Logs
*.log

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Editor / IDE
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# Claude Code local settings & Plan C agent-runtime (machine-local, not committed)
.claude/
CLAUDE.md
.mcp.json

# Stray build output: `go build ./internal/orchestrator/testdata/fakeshim`
# writes the binary into the working directory unless -o says otherwise.
/fakeshim

# Generated documentation site (built by internal/site; never committed)
_site_src/
_site/

# What opossum writes about a project it is running (MCP wiring, mount-failure
# records, workspace snapshots). Read back at run time, so a committed copy is
# state a fresh clone would act on. The tracked-file gate refuses it too — this
# is the half that keeps it from being offered.
.opossum/
